  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of how microglia recognize and engulf stressed or damaged neurons. It centers on the idea that neuronal stress, triggered by damage, pathogens, or amyloid-β, causes the neuron to expose phosphatidylserine on its outer membrane, creating an “eat-me” signal for nearby microglia.\n\nInside the neuron, stress appears to activate caspases, reduce ATP, alter calcium, and generate reactive oxygen or nitrogen species, which in turn regulate membrane-scrambling proteins such as XKR8, ATP8A, and TMEM16. These changes shift phosphatidylserine to the cell surface. The diagram also suggests that additional opsonins and eat-me signals may participate.\n\nMicroglia detect these cues through several receptor systems. TLR signaling is shown as an upstream activator in response to damage-related stimuli. Exposed phosphatidylserine is linked to the opsonin MFG-E8, which bridges the neuron to microglial vitronectin receptors, while MERTK provides another recognition pathway. These receptor interactions converge on actin polymerization within the microglial process, shown by F-actin filaments, implying the cytoskeletal remodeling needed for phagocytosis. Overall, the figure illustrates a coordinated neuron–microglia pathway in which neuronal distress marks the cell for clearance and microglia respond by initiating engulfment.",
